   "html_text": "Despite this impasse, new research continues to be conducted on false belief attribution in animals. In particular, Krupenye, Kano, et al. (2016) adapted eye-tracking methods from infant cognition to provide evidence that chimpanzees, bonobos, and orangutans can anticipate that others will act according to false beliefs. While many experimenters had considered applying a version of the canonical Sally-Anne test to animals, it had been difficult to find a non-linguistic analogue that could be app",
